course: dictionary
lesson: 1606
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so context decides which meaning you need. Read the words around the word first, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: «Tara-a-bit means goodbye for now in Birmingham and the Black Country; cheerio is a goodbye heard across Britain.» Both words are goodbyes, so the clue is the word goodbye. Cheerio is a friendly, informal way to say goodbye.
explain: Use the swap test: put goodbye in place of cheerio. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning. A party sentence about cheerios is different, because there cheerios are small red sausages.
fact: Cheerio means goodbye, said in a friendly, cheerful way (UK, informal).
fact: Cheerio is informal, so friends and family say it. It is not a formal word.
fact: In Australia, a cheerio can be a small red cocktail sausage, often served at children's parties.
fact: Cheerios is also the name of a breakfast cereal shaped like little rings.
fact: In some places, the small sausage is also called a little boy or a saveloy.
exercise: What does cheerio mean when someone says it as they leave? | A bowl of breakfast cereal | Sorry for the mess | Hello, come in | *Goodbye, said in a friendly way
exercise: Ella sees cheerios on the party table in Australia. What are they? | A goodbye card | Rings of breakfast cereal | A type of cake | *Small red cocktail sausages
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to cheerio as a goodbye? | *Bye | Party | Ring | Breakfast
test: Where is cheerio heard as a goodbye? | Only at children's parties | Only in Australia | Nowhere in the UK | *Across Britain
test: The word cheerio is informal. What does informal mean? | Written in old books only | Very formal, used in official letters | Only used by teachers | *Relaxed, the way friends talk
exam: Which sentence uses cheerio to mean goodbye? | Cheerios with milk are my favourite breakfast | The red cheerios were served at the party | *"Cheerio, see you tomorrow!" said Grandma from the door | The sausage was small and red
